  "question": {
    "text": "The internal energy of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;mol of hydrogen of temperature&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math> is equal to the internal energy of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;mol of helium at temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi></math>. What will be the value of the ratio&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></math>?"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "1.2",
  "solution": {
    "text": "The internal energy of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math> moles of an ideal gas at temperature T is given by<br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext mathvariant=\"italic\">U</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mtext mathvariant=\"italic\">nRT</mtext></math>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(<math> <mrow> <mi>f</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo> <mo>=</mo> </mrow> </math>&nbsp;degrees of freedom)<br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math><br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><msub><mo>&#8289;</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mo>&#8289;</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math><br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;&#160;&#160;&#160;</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>f</mi><msub><mo>&#8289;</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><msub><mo>&#8289;</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn></math><br /><br />Here, <math> <mrow> <msub> <mrow> <mi>f</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo> </mrow> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math> = degrees of freedom of He = 3<br /><br />and, <math> <mrow> <msub> <mrow> <mi>f</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo> </mrow> <mn>1</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math>&nbsp; = degrees of freedom of H<sub>2</sub> = 5"
  }
}